        <div id="how-to-apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Sprayer Technique for Large Fence Runs
          </h2>
          <p>
            Brushing a 300-foot fence by hand is a multi-weekend project and, on penetrating oils especially, a good way to introduce inconsistent coverage. Every professional fence-sealing crew we've watched works with a sprayer, and the technique below is what separated an even, well-protected fence from a streaky one in our own testing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: Choose the Right Sprayer for the Job</h3>
          <p>
            For a full privacy or shadowbox fence, an <strong>airless sprayer</strong> with a 0.013-0.017 inch tip covers ground fastest and atomizes penetrating oils evenly. For picket or split-rail fences with lots of individual spindles and gaps, a <strong>pump-up garden sprayer</strong> gives you more control and less overspray onto grass, siding, or a neighbor's car. Whichever you use, strain the sealer through a paint filter first — even a small amount of debris will clog a fine tip mid-run.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Mask and Protect Before You Start</h3>
          <p>
            Overspray travels farther than you expect, especially in any wind. Cover nearby plants, hardscaping, house siding, and any concrete footings or post bases you don't want sealed with plastic sheeting or drop cloths. If your fence borders a shared property line, give your neighbor a heads-up — spray drift doesn't respect fence lines any better than the fence itself does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Work in Consistent Sections, Both Sides</h3>
          <p>
            Break the run into manageable sections — we used roughly 8-10 foot segments — and spray top to bottom in a steady pass, holding the tip about 12 inches from the wood. Immediately back-roll or back-brush water-based and thin sealers to work the product into the grain and knock down runs; true penetrating oils like Ready Seal are more forgiving and can often be left to self-level. Complete one full side of the fence before starting the other, and plan for both faces on privacy and shadowbox styles — the backside is just as exposed to weather as the front.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 4: Don't Skip the Top and Bottom Edges</h3>
          <p>
            End grain at the top of pickets and the bottom of boards near the ground absorbs water far faster than the flat face of a board, and it's the spot most sprayer passes miss because the gun is aimed at the broad surface. Tip the sprayer up and down briefly at each section to catch cap boards, post tops, and the bottom few inches of pickets — these are usually the first places a fence starts to rot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 5: Check Coverage in Raking Light</h3>
          <p>
            Walk the fence line at a low angle to the sun after each section — missed or thin spots show up as dull patches next to the wet sheen of properly coated wood. Go back over any dry-looking areas before moving on; it's far easier to catch a miss immediately than to spot it after the whole fence has cured.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 6: Respect Cure Time on Both Sides</h3>
          <p>
            Keep sprinklers off and pets away from freshly sealed sections for at least 24-48 hours, and check the manufacturer's rain-safe window before spraying if weather is in the forecast — an unexpected downpour on a still-wet coat can wash out a full day's work on a long fence run.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Surface Prep Before Sealing
          </h2>
          <p>
            Prep matters more on a clear sealer than on a stain, because there's no pigment to mask an uneven job — every streak, missed spot, or patch of trapped dirt shows through a clear finish more visibly than it would under color.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Clean Both Faces</h3>
          <p>
            Wash the fence with a wood-safe cleaner or a diluted oxygen-bleach solution, scrubbing dirt, pollen, and mildew off both faces — not just the side you look at from the yard. Rinse with a garden hose or a pressure washer kept under about 1,500 PSI to avoid gouging softer woods like cedar.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Strip Any Old Finish</h3>
          <p>
            If the fence has a failing old sealer or stain, it needs to come off before a new clear sealer can penetrate — a wood stripper or a more aggressive pressure wash pass, followed by neutralizing per the stripper's instructions. Sealing over a peeling old finish just locks the problem in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Let It Dry Fully</h3>
          <p>
            New pressure-treated lumber needs 4-8 weeks to dry to the point stain and sealer will penetrate; freshly washed fencing needs at least 24-48 hours of dry weather. Confirm with the water-drop test — sprinkle a few drops on the wood, and if it beads instead of soaking in within a couple minutes, the wood isn't ready yet.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Fence-Sealing Mistakes We See Most Often</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-red-900 text-sm">
              <li>• Sealing only the visible face and skipping the neighbor-facing side</li>
              <li>• Spraying over wet or still-drying wood after washing</li>
              <li>• Missing end grain at post tops, cap boards, and picket bottoms</li>
              <li>• Sealing over a peeling old finish instead of stripping it first</li>
              <li>• Using a fine sprayer tip with unstrained sealer, causing clogs mid-run</li>
              <li>• Expecting a fully clear sealer to stop graying the way a tinted stain would</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="by-fence-style" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            5. Sealer Needs by Fence Style
          </h2>
          <p>
            Not every fence style demands the same approach. How much surface area you're covering, whether both faces matter, and how exposed the wood is all shift depending on the style you own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Picket fences</strong> are short, open, and usually seen from both sides since there's no privacy panel — every picket face and every bit of end grain at the top is exposed to full sun and rain. Coverage area is modest, so a clear sealer's shorter recoat interval is easy to live with, and a pump sprayer or even a brush is manageable for most yards.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Privacy fences</strong> are where the sprayer technique in this guide matters most — tall, solid panels running continuously for hundreds of feet with two full faces to coat. This is the style where cost per linear foot, sprayer efficiency, and choosing a product that covers both sides economically (Olympic Maximum or Thompson's for budget, Ready Seal or Seal-Once for longevity) becomes a real planning decision.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Split-rail fences</strong> have minimal surface area but every face of every rail and post sits in full sun with no panel to provide any shade — mildew is rarely the issue, UV fade is. A sealer with at least some UV additive, like DEFY Crystal Clear, holds up better here than a fully clear product with none.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Shadowbox fences</strong> alternate boards on each side, meaning both faces are visible and both take weather directly, similar to a privacy fence but with more edge and end-grain exposure at each overlap. These benefit most from a sealer with strong penetration into rough-sawn texture, like Rain Guard Woodland Premium or Seal-Once Marine Premium, since the overlapping boards create more nooks where water can sit.
          </p>
        </div>

