        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the difference between a fence sealer and a fence stain?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A sealer is clear or nearly clear and adds water repellency without changing the wood's color, so the fence keeps its natural or weathered tone. A stain adds pigment that tints the wood a specific color while also adding some water protection. If you want the fence to look like unfinished or naturally weathered wood, choose a clear or semi-clear sealer like Ready Seal Clear or DEFY Crystal Clear. If you want to change or even out the wood's color, choose a tinted stain instead.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a clear sealer protect a fence from UV as well as a tinted stain?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">No, not fully. Pigment is what actually blocks ultraviolet light, so a truly clear sealer with zero tint will let the wood gray faster than a tinted stain, even a lightly tinted one. Sealers like DEFY Crystal Clear and Rain Guard Woodland Premium close part of that gap with zinc-oxide UV additives or a faint semi-clear tint, but a fully clear finish should be understood as a water-repellent product first and a UV shield second.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to spray or brush a fence sealer on a large fence?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">For anything beyond a short run of fence, spraying is dramatically faster and more even than brushing or rolling. An airless or pump sprayer lets you cover hundreds of linear feet in a fraction of the time, especially with penetrating oils that self-level and don't require back-brushing. Back-roll or back-brush immediately after spraying on water-based or thinner formulas to work the product into the grain. A brush alone is still the right tool for gate hardware, post caps, and tight lattice detail.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to seal both sides of a wood fence?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, ideally. Privacy and shadowbox fences take weather on both faces, and the side you don't see as often is frequently the shadier, damper side where mildew and rot start first. Sealing only the visible side leaves the unsealed face absorbing rain and staying wet longer. Budget for roughly double the sealer when planning a two-sided privacy fence versus a one-sided picket or split-rail fence.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to seal a fence per linear foot?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Sealing a standard 6-foot privacy fence typically runs $1 to $3 per linear foot in materials when both sides are coated, depending on the product and how porous the wood is. Budget sealers like Thompson's or Olympic Maximum sit at the low end, while premium hybrid or oil-based sealers like Seal-Once Marine Premium can run closer to $3 to $4 per linear foot for both faces. Picket and split-rail fences cost less per foot since there's less surface area and often only one side to coat.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a wood fence be resealed?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Most clear and semi-clear sealers need reapplication every 1 to 2 years on a fully sun-exposed fence, compared to 2 to 3 years for a tinted semi-transparent stain, because clear formulas carry less UV-blocking pigment. Premium hybrid sealers like Seal-Once Marine Premium extended that window closer to 2 years in our testing. Shaded fence sections and split-rail fences with less continuous sun exposure typically stretch the interval longer than a south-facing privacy fence panel.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

